<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sybaris</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sybaris was a Greek city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded by Achaean colonists around 720 BCE and so wealthy that its name became a byword for luxury. It was destroyed by its rival Croton in 510 BCE. Descendants of its citizens took part in the Panhellenic foundation of Thurii nearby in 444/443 BCE but were soon expelled after quarrels with the other settlers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sybaris.jpg" alt="Sybaris" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mboesch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sibari-scavi-teatro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sybaris lay on the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-taranto">Gulf of Taranto</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, between the rivers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crati">Crathis</a> and Sybaris, now the Crati and Coscile. The plain around it is the widest in Calabria and was noted for its fertility, the source of the city's wealth. It lay close to sea level and flooded periodically, so that the site now lies about 6 metres below the surface and below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>. A flood in 2013 filled the excavated area with silt, and pumps are still needed to keep it dry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sybaris was founded around 720 BCE by Achaean settlers from Helice, accompanied by Troezenians whom the more numerous Achaeans eventually expelled. It grew rich on its farmland and by admitting foreigners to its citizenship, and may have had 300,000 inhabitants, though others give 100,000. It ruled four tribes and 25 cities, and founded colonies on the Tyrrhenian coast, among them Poseidonia, around 600 BCE. It began to mint coins around 530 BCE. Ancient writers told of the luxury of citizens such as Smindyrides, who, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, surpassed all other men in refined luxury.</p><p>In 510/509 BCE, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, a popular leader named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telys">Telys</a> exiled the 500 richest citizens, who took refuge at Kroton. Pythagoras persuaded the Krotoniates to protect them, and their army under Milo defeated the Sybarites and razed the city. Walter Burkert doubts this account. Sybaris was not wholly destroyed, but became a dependent ally of Kroton, and its people left the site again between 476 and 452/451 BCE. The Sybarites refounded it in 452/451 BCE and were driven out in 446/445 BCE. Athenian colonists then came and in 444/443 BCE founded Thurii. The Sybarites quarrelled with the other settlers, and most were killed. Thurii was destroyed in 193 BCE and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built Copia on the same grid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sediment from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crati">Crati</a>, more than 6 metres deep, buried the ruins, and the site was located only through a large core-drilling project. Because of the depth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>, only the Stombi quarter and small test pits in the Parco del Cavallo have been dug, the latter yielding decorated architectural elements from an unidentified temple. The ruins lie southeast of Sibari in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosenza">Cosenza</a>. The sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> on the Timpone della Motta near Francavilla Marittima, taken over by the Sybarites as their acropolis, is another source of evidence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sybaris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Colonists from Sybaris founded Poseidonia around 600 BCE on the plain near the mouth of the Sele river, giving <b>Sybaris</b> an outlet on the Tyrrhenian Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/poseidonia">Poseidonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Siris was famous for its fertile land and luxury, like its neighbour Sybaris, and became a rival to the Achaean cities of Metapontum, <b>Sybaris</b> and Croton.</blockquote><a href="/state/siris">Siris</a></li><li><blockquote>The settlers soon quarrelled; the descendants of <b>Sybarites</b> claimed privileges and were expelled, and the citizen body was organised in ten tribes by origin.</blockquote><a href="/state/thurii">Thurii</a></li></ul></section>
